{"sections":[],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"PlanarEmbedding"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"G"},{"kind":"text","text":"> "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"where"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"G"},{"kind":"text","text":" : "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P","text":"Graph"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"overview","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A self-loop appears twice in its vertex’s order, its two ends adjacent, enclosing a face of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"length 1. Parallel copies sit side by side, each consecutive pair enclosing a face of length 2."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Each clockwise order starts at the first edge of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdges(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"Faces.","type":"text"}]},{"text":" The face after the half-edge from u along edge e to w continues with the half-edge","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"before e in the clockwise order at w (NetworkX’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"next_face_half_edge","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"), so a face lies to the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"right of each of its half-edges. Faces are counted per connected component, and an isolated","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex has none: two disjoint triangles have four faces.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The embedding keeps the graph it was made from (copy-on-write, so O(1)); mutating the graph","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"afterwards does not change it. When a graph has several embeddings, which one is returned is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"not specified, but it depends only on the graph’s rows: graphs with the same vertices, the same","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edge positions and the same incidence order get the same embedding.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"kind":"symbol","metadata":{"symbolKind":"struct","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"PlanarEmbedding"}],"remoteSource":{"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/Planarity\/PlanarEmbedding.swift#L67","fileName":"PlanarEmbedding.swift"},"title":"PlanarEmbedding","modules":[{"name":"Planarity"}],"role":"symbol","externalID":"s:9Planarity15PlanarEmbeddingV","roleHeading":"Structure","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"PlanarEmbedding"}]},"abstract":[{"text":"A planar embedding of a graph: a rotation system, the clockwise order of the edges around each","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex, over every edge position (NetworkX’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"PlanarEmbedding","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", LEMON’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"PlanarEmbedding","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"OGDF’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"CombinatorialEmbedding","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"). Its faces satisfy Euler’s formula, n − m + f = 2, in every","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"connected component with an edge.","type":"text"}],"topicSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/PlanarEmbedding\/ClockwiseIncidentEdges","doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/PlanarEmbedding\/ClockwiseNeighborIndices","doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planari